.TH TEE 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tee \- copy standard input to standard output and files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tee
.RB [ \-ai ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tee
reads standard input and writes everything it reads both to standard
output and to each
.IR file .
Files are created with mode 0666 (modified by the umask) and truncated
unless
.B \-a
is given.
.PP
Short writes and interrupted system calls are retried.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is diagnosed and skipped; a
.I file
that fails on write is diagnosed, closed and not written again.
In both cases copying continues to the other destinations and the exit
status becomes nonzero.
.PP
Option processing stops at the first argument that does not begin with
.BR \- ,
at a lone
.B \-
(which is treated as an ordinary file name), or after
.BR \-\- .
Short options may be combined, as in
.BR \-ai .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-append
Append to each
.I file
instead of truncating it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-interrupts
Ignore
.BR SIGINT .
.TP
.B \-p
Accepted for compatibility; has no effect.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage line and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input was copied to every destination.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option was given, or an error occurred reading input,
writing standard output, or opening, writing or closing a
.IR file .
.SH EXAMPLE
Save a build log while watching it:
.EX
$ make 2>&1 | tee \-a build.log
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR open (2),
.BR write (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024, with the GNU long options as extensions.
